Rule O1 (doc/scope_model.mld, sections 3.1 and 7): when odoc copies a term
from one unit into another, every root in it keeps its origin, the unit whose
source it came from. A root that odoc makes from another occurrence, for
example when it strengthens a path, takes that occurrence's origin. This file
tests the operations that substitute into a term: functor application,
[with module], and strengthening, and the paths that functor application
unresolves and resolves again.

Libraries [ext1] and [ext2] each have a module [Ext], with different
contents. [x] and [y] are dune wrapped libraries built against [ext1]. Their
alias modules [X] and [Y] are generated by dune, and their members are
compiled with [-open X] or [-open Y]. [x] has the members [Fn], [Wt] and
[Hid], and [y] has [W]. The pure alias [E = Ext] appears in [Fn]'s functors,
in [Hid] and in [W], so [x] and [y] record no digest for [Ext] (rule P2,
section 3.2). [z] is built against [x], [y] and [ext1]. Each unit of [z] uses
one operation.

No library here is built against [ext2], so no unit sees two [Ext]s (D3,
section 2). Under D3, [z] can only see the [Ext] that [x] and [y] see, so
these cases cannot tell the scope of [x] from the scope of [z]. What they can
show is that the root keeps a scope at all. [ext2] is given at link, like
every library. A root that lost its origin would be looked up among all the
libraries given at link, and would find two [Ext]s.

  $ cat x/x.ml-gen
  (** @canonical X.Fn *)
  module Fn = X__Fn
  
  (** @canonical X.Wt *)
  module Wt = X__Wt
  
  (** @canonical X.Hid *)
  module Hid = X__Hid
  $ cat x/hid.mli
  module E = Ext
  $ cat y/y.ml-gen
  (** @canonical Y.W *)
  module W = Y__W
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -c ext1/ext.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -c ext2/ext.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -w -49 -I x -I ext1 -o x/x -c -impl x/x.ml-gen
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I x -I ext1 -open X -o x/x__Fn -c -intf x/fn.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I x -I ext1 -open X -o x/x__Wt -c -intf x/wt.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I x -I ext1 -open X -o x/x__Hid -c -intf x/hid.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -w -49 -I y -I ext1 -o y/y -c -impl y/y.ml-gen
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I y -I ext1 -open Y -o y/y__W -c -intf y/w.mli
  $ for m in apply opaque with strong; do
  >   ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I z -I x -I y -I ext1 -c z/$m.mli
  > done

odoc compiles each unit after the units it depends on (D5).

  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/ext1 -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 ext1/ext.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/ext2 -L ext2:h/pkg/ext2 ext2/ext.cmti
  $ for u in x__Fn.cmti x__Wt.cmti x__Hid.cmti x.cmt; do
  >   odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/x -L x:h/pkg/x -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 x/$u
  > done
  $ for u in y__W.cmti y.cmt; do
  >   odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/y -L y:h/pkg/y -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 y/$u
  > done
  $ for m in apply opaque with strong; do
  >   odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/z -L z:h/pkg/z -L x:h/pkg/x -L y:h/pkg/y -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 z/$m.cmti
  > done

  $ link() { odoc link -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 -L ext2:h/pkg/ext2 -L x:h/pkg/x -L y:h/pkg/y -L z:h/pkg/z "$@"; }
  $ show() { odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents --show-expansions --show-include-expansions "$@"; }

How to read [odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents]. Each line
is one item of the signature. [x/0] is the item [x], with a number odoc adds
to tell apart items of the same name. [resolved(...)] is a resolved path, and
[root(pkg/ext1/Ext)] is the unit [Ext] of the library [ext1] in the package
[pkg]. [r(...)] is a resolved module type path. [canonical(p,resolved(q))]
is a path [p] whose canonical path is [q], and [hidden(...)] marks a path to
a hidden unit. [substituted(P/1)] is the functor argument [P/1] put in place
of the parameter, [opaquemodule(...)] a path to a module whose module
type is abstract, and [opaquemoduletype(...)] a path to an abstract module
type. [alias(p,q)] is the path [q], which is an alias to [p].
[identifier(i,false)] is the identifier [i] used as a path, and [false] says
that it is not hidden. With [--show-expansions] and [--show-include-expansions], [=>] is followed by
the signature odoc computed for a module or an [include].

Case 1. Functor application. [Fn.F]'s result has the module type [R], which has
[E = Ext] and [u = A.t]. [Apply] has [M : X.Fn.F(P).R], so odoc applies [F]
to [Apply]'s [P]. The roots from [F]'s body have the origin [X__Fn], so [E]
should be [ext1]'s [Ext]. The argument was written in [Apply], so [u] should
be [Apply]'s [P.t]. There should be no warning.

  $ cat z/apply.mli
  module P : X.Fn.S
  
  module M : X.Fn.F(P).R
  $ cat ext1/ext.mli
  type t = A
  $ cat ext2/ext.mli
  type t = B
  $ cat x/fn.mli
  module type S = sig
    type t
  end
  
  module F (A : S) : sig
    module type R = sig
      module E = Ext
  
      type u = A.t
    end
  end
  
  module type T
  
  module type U = sig
    module O : T
  end
  
  module G (A : U) : sig
    module type R = sig
      module E = Ext
  
      module O : module type of A.O
    end
  end
  $ link h/pkg/z/apply.odoc
  $ show h/pkg/z/apply.odocl
  module P/1 : r(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Fn,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Fn)).S)
    => sig type t/2 end
  module M/0 : r(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Fn,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Fn)).F(P/1).R)
    => sig
      module E/4 = resolved(root(pkg/ext1/Ext))
      type u/3 = resolved(substituted(P/1).t)
     end

Case 2. Unresolve and resolve again. [Fn.G]'s parameter has a module [O] whose
module type [T] is abstract, so a path to [A.O] is opaque. When odoc applies
[G], it unresolves such paths and resolves them again in the result. [Opaque]
has [M : X.Fn.G(Q).R]. The [O] in [M] should be [Opaque]'s [Q.O], and [E]
should still be [ext1]'s [Ext], with no warning:

  $ cat z/opaque.mli
  module Q : X.Fn.U
  
  module M : X.Fn.G(Q).R
  $ link h/pkg/z/opaque.odoc
  $ show h/pkg/z/opaque.odocl
  module Q/1 : r(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Fn,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Fn)).U)
    => sig
      module O/2 : r(opaquemoduletype(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Fn,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Fn)).T))
     end
  module M/0 : r(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Fn,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Fn)).G(Q/1).R)
    => sig
      module E/4 = resolved(root(pkg/ext1/Ext))
      module O/3 : module type of resolved(opaquemodule(substituted(Q/1).O))
     end

Case 3. [with module M = A], where [A] comes from a third library. [Wt.S] is
written in [x], the [with] in [z], and [A] is [y]'s [W]. [With] has [M :
X.Wt.S with module M = Y__W], which names [y]'s hidden unit, so the link
shows [Y__W]'s expansion. Its [E = Ext] was written in [y], so [E] should be
[ext1]'s [Ext], with no warning:

  $ cat z/with.mli
  module M : X.Wt.S with module M = Y__W
  $ cat x/wt.mli
  module type S = sig
    module M : sig
      module E : sig
        type t
      end
    end
  end
  $ cat y/w.mli
  module E = Ext
  $ link h/pkg/z/with.odoc
  $ show h/pkg/z/with.odocl
  module M/0 : r(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Wt,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Wt)).S) with 
    resolved(root(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Wt,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Wt)).S).M) =
      resolved(hidden(root(pkg/y/Y__W)))
    => sig
      module M/1 = resolved(hidden(root(pkg/y/Y__W)))
        => sig module E/2 = resolved(root(pkg/ext1/Ext)) end
     end

Case 4. Strengthening. [Strong] has [include module type of struct include
X.Hid.E end]. odoc recomputes the signature of [X.Hid.E] and strengthens it
with that path. [X.Hid.E] is the alias [E = Ext], written in [x], so its
signature is [ext1]'s [Ext], with the constructor [A]. The new path in the
manifest of [t] comes from [X.Hid.E], written in [Strong], so it should mean
[ext1]'s [Ext.t]. There should be no warning:

  $ cat z/strong.mli
  include module type of struct
    include X.Hid.E
  end
  $ link h/pkg/z/strong.odoc
  $ show h/pkg/z/strong.odocl
  include module type of struct include resolved(alias(root(pkg/ext1/Ext),identifier(root(pkg/x/X),false).Hid.E)) end
    => sig type t/0 : resolved(root(pkg/ext1/Ext).t) = A end
